{# The two controls that belong to the VIEWER rather than to the wiki: which Colour scheme
   they read it in, and which language they read it in (ADR-0020).

   They are core's and they sit apart from the quick menu on purpose. The quick menu is what
   an admin puts there; these two are not, and must not be — a page whose colours or language
   a visitor cannot change is a page some visitors cannot read, so removing them is not a
   choice a wiki gets to make. Hence the divider: what is to the left of it is this wiki's,
   what is to the right of it is YesWiki's.

   Collapsed, each is a mark saying what is on. The choices arrive **in their own column**
   when one is reached for, overlaying the bar rather than taking room in it: a row of
   controls permanently in the navbar would be the loudest thing on it, and a line that
   expanded in flow would shove the account button sideways on every hover. Below 768px there
   is nothing to hover with, so the choices are simply part of the bar (see yw-core.css).

   TWO dropdowns, not one panel holding both. They answer different questions -- how the page
   is coloured, and what language it is in -- and a reader opening one has no business being
   shown the other. As one panel they read as a single run of six marks, and the rule drawn
   between the halves was doing the work a separate menu does properly. #}
<div class="yw-topnav-tools">
  {# ---- how it is coloured ---- #}
  <div class="yw-topnav-tools__menu">
    <div class="yw-topnav-tools__panel">
      {# Three states, not two: "follow my system" is a choice of its own, and it is the one a
         visitor who has never touched this is already in. Radios rather than a cycling button
         so reaching the third is one click from anywhere rather than three. #}
      {# the labels are written out rather than built from the state name: `_t('SCHEME_' ~
         scheme|upper)` would work and would be invisible to every tool that finds translation
         keys by grepping for them #}
      <div class="yw-switcher" role="group" aria-label="{{ _t('SCHEME_TOGGLE') }}">
        {% for scheme, option in {
          'system': { glyph: 'device-desktop', label: _t('SCHEME_SYSTEM') },
          'light': { glyph: 'sun', label: _t('SCHEME_LIGHT') },
          'dark': { glyph: 'moon', label: _t('SCHEME_DARK') },
        } %}
          <button type="button" class="yw-switcher__option" data-yw-scheme-set="{{ scheme }}"
                  title="{{ option.label }}" aria-pressed="false">
            {{ icon(option.glyph) }}
            <span class="yw-switcher__label">{{ option.label }}</span>
          </button>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>

    {# what is on, without asking: the scheme's own glyph (yw-core.js keeps it current) #}
    <span class="yw-topnav-tools__current" data-yw-scheme aria-hidden="true"
          data-label-system="{{ _t('SCHEME_SYSTEM') }}"
          data-label-light="{{ _t('SCHEME_LIGHT') }}"
          data-label-dark="{{ _t('SCHEME_DARK') }}">
      {{ icon('device-desktop') }}
    </span>
  </div>

  {# ---- what language it is in ----
     Only where there is something to choose between. A wiki says which languages it offers
     (`other_languages`, chosen at install time and on the configuration screen), and one is
     the ordinary answer: that wiki gets no menu at all, and no readout of a language nobody
     can change either. #}
  {% if languages|length > 1 %}
    <div class="yw-topnav-tools__menu">
      <div class="yw-topnav-tools__panel">
        <div class="yw-switcher" role="group" aria-label="{{ _t('LANGUAGE_CHOOSE') }}">
          {% for language in languages %}
            {# links, not buttons: the language is a property of the address (`lang=`), so
               each one is a real page a reader can bookmark, open in a tab, or share #}
            <a class="yw-switcher__option{{ language.current ? ' yw-switcher__option--on' : '' }}"
               href="{{ language.href }}" hreflang="{{ language.code }}" lang="{{ language.code }}"
               title="{{ language.label }}"{{ language.current ? ' aria-current="true"' : '' }}>
              <span class="yw-switcher__code">{{ language.code }}</span>
              <span class="yw-switcher__label">{{ language.label }}</span>
            </a>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </div>

      <span class="yw-topnav-tools__current" aria-hidden="true" title="{{ _t('LANGUAGE_CHOOSE') }}">
        <span class="yw-switcher__code">{{ language|upper }}</span>
      </span>
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>
